\title{Spokes at a vertex}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{definition}
\meta{defines}{spoke; outward spoke; spoke direction}
\meta{dependencies}{[Complete bipartite graphs](cn-0002); [Continuous drawings](cn-0003)}
\meta{provenance}{Terminology used in [OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Section 3 (before equation (3.1) and in the proof of Lemma 3.1) and Appendix A (proof of Lemma A.1); explanatory definition added on 28 October 2026. [Lean source: TruncatedSpokeDirections](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/TruncatedSpokeDirections.lean) defines, for a vertex truncation of a polygonal drawing, the boundary point (spokePort) and the direction (spokeDirection) of the first or last polygonal piece of an edge at a vertex.}

\p{Consider a [continuous plane drawing](cn-0003) in which every edge is straight near each of its endpoints, as in a normalized drawing. Let #{v} be a vertex and #{e} an incident edge. A \em{spoke} of #{e} at #{v} is a straight segment of the arc of #{e} with one endpoint #{v}, short enough to lie in the part of #{e} that is straight near #{v}. For #{e=ip} it is #{\gamma_{ip}([0,\varepsilon])} at #{a_i} or #{\gamma_{ip}([1-\varepsilon,1])} at #{b_p}, for small #{\varepsilon>0}. It is regarded as directed away from #{v}, and is therefore also called an \em{outward spoke}. Its \em{spoke direction} is the unit vector #{u_{ve}} pointing from #{v} along it; it does not depend on the chosen length.}

\p{The word outward refers to the vertex, not to the fixed orientation of #{ip} from #{a_i} to #{b_p} in [#{K_{m,n}}](cn-0002). At #{a_i} the outward spoke of #{ip} points along that orientation; at #{b_p} it points against it. The sign #{\sigma_{ve}} of [spoke orders](cn-0011) records this difference separately. The Lean declaration spokeDirection cited in the provenance keeps the fixed orientation of the edge and is not normalized; writing it #{d_{ve}}, the outward unit direction used here is #{u_{ve}=\sigma_{ve}d_{ve}/\|d_{ve}\|}.}

\p{The paper uses small round disks around the vertices that meet edges only in straight spokes: a closed disk centered at #{v} of radius #{r>0} which every incident edge meets exactly in its spoke of length #{r}, and which no other edge meets. For sufficiently small #{r} such disks exist and are pairwise disjoint, because each edge is straight near its endpoints, the rest of each incident edge is compact and avoids #{v}, and nonincident edges avoid #{v}. Inside such a disk the incident edges are radii. When distinct incident edges have distinct spoke directions, as in a [normalized drawing](cn-0008), the counterclockwise order of these directions is the order read in [spoke orders](cn-0011). For an [admissible drawing](cn-0004) this is automatic: two incident edges with equal spoke directions would share an initial segment, which admissibility excludes. In the [normalization proof](cn-0009) the word is used in the same sense for the straight segments joining a new vertex point to the boundary points of a vertex disk.}
